# Frontend environment files
#
# Next.js loads the following files automatically (in increasing
# precedence):
#   .env                  ← lowest priority; loaded for every command.
#   .env.development      ← loaded by `next dev` (local development).
#   .env.production       ← loaded by `next build` / `next start`
#                           (used by the Docker build inside
#                           `Dockerfile.frontend`).
#   .env.local            ← highest priority; local-only overrides.
#                           Always gitignored.
#
# See https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/env.
#
# This repository ships:
#   - .env.development  → http://localhost:5180  (dotnet run)
#   - .env.production   → http://localhost:8080  (compose backend port)
# Both files are committed because they contain no secrets. Override them
# per-developer via .env.local (also gitignored) when needed.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables are inlined into the static bundle at build
# time by Next.js. The value is consumed by `src/config/runtime.ts`
# (browser side, no secret material) and by `next.config.mjs` (build-time
# only). Backend-only secrets (JWT signing key, SQL Server password,
# ...) must NEVER be exposed as `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` and must live in
# environment variables consumed by the backend container only.
#
# Examples (use as a template for your own .env.local):
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5180   # `dotnet run` profile (local dev)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080   # docker compose (backend: 8080:8080)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com # custom deployment
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5180
